# Problem 1. Prediction design and leakage control

## 1A. Target and predictors

```{r data-configuration, include = FALSE}
config <- if (group_number %% 2L == 1L) {
  list(
    file = "fat.csv",
    response = "brozek",
    excluded = c("brozek", "siri", "density", "free")
  )
} else {
  list(
    file = "prostate.csv",
    response = "lpsa",
    excluded = "lpsa"
  )
}

data_raw <- read.csv(find_exam_data(config$file), check.names = FALSE)
predictors <- setdiff(names(data_raw), config$excluded)
analysis_data <- data_raw[, c(config$response, predictors), drop = FALSE]

```

### Dataset Overview

The analysis uses the **Prostate Cancer** dataset, which contains clinical measurements collected from men with prostate cancer. The dataset was introduced by Stamey et al. (1989) [@stamey1989] to investigate the relationship between prostate-specific antigen (PSA) levels and a set of clinical variables associated with prostate cancer progression.

```{r dataset-overview, echo=FALSE}
dataset_overview <- tibble::tibble(
  Property = c(
    "Dataset",
    "Observations",
    "Variables"
  ),
  Value = c(
    config$file,
    nrow(data_raw),
    ncol(data_raw)
  )
)

render_table(
  dataset_overview, 
  "Overview of the Prostate Cancer Dataset",
  "cc"
)

```

### Response Variable and Candidate Predictors

- The response variable is **lpsa**, which represents the natural logarithm of the prostate-specific antigen (PSA) level.

- The candidate predictors consist of the remaining clinical variables, including measurements of tumour volume, prostate weight, age, benign prostatic hyperplasia, seminal vesicle invasion, capsular penetration, Gleason score, and the percentage of Gleason grades 4 or 5.

```{r variable, echo = FALSE}
variable_summary <- tibble::tibble(
  Variable = names(data_raw),
  Type = vapply(data_raw, function(x) class(x)[1], character(1)),
  Role = ifelse(names(data_raw) == config$response,
                "Response",
                "Predictor")
)

render_table(
  variable_summary,
  "Summary of Variables in the Prostate Dataset",
  "cc"
)

```

### Study Population and Unit of Observation

The study population consists of patients with prostate cancer who underwent radical prostatectomy. The unit of observation is a single patient. Each observation contains one response variable (*lpsa*) and a set of clinical predictors describing the patient's tumour characteristics and disease severity.

### Prediction Objective

The objective of this study is to develop predictive regression models for estimating the response variable **lpsa**, which represents the logarithm of the prostate-specific antigen (PSA) level, using the available clinical predictors. Accurate prediction of PSA levels can provide insights into the relationship between clinical characteristics and prostate cancer severity.

### Required Exclusions

According to the assignment requirements, the response variable **lpsa** is excluded from the set of candidate predictors. Consequently, the predictor matrix is constructed using only the remaining clinical variables.

In predictive modelling, the response variable must never be included as a predictor because it would provide the model with direct access to the value being predicted. This results in **target leakage**, leading to unrealistically optimistic estimates of predictive performance.

More generally, any **outcome-derived variable** should also be excluded. An outcome-derived variable is a variable that is calculated from, or directly depends on, the response variable. Including such variables would introduce information that would not be available when making predictions for new observations. As a result, model comparisons would become invalid because the estimated prediction errors would no longer reflect the true generalization performance of the models.

## 1B. Split and train-only preprocessing

### Random Train-Test Split 

The dataset was randomly partitioned into training and test sets using a fixed random seed (*`r seeds$split`*). Approximately 80% of the observations were assigned to the training set, while the remaining 20% formed the holdout test set. The test set was reserved exclusively for the final model evaluation and was not used during model fitting, feature selection, hyperparameter tuning, or preprocessing.

```{r split, echo = FALSE}
split <- split_rows(
    n = nrow(analysis_data),
    train_prop = 0.80,
    seed = seeds$split
)

train_id <- split$train
test_id <- split$test

stopifnot(
    length(intersect(train_id, test_id)) == 0L
)

split_summary <- tibble::tibble(
  Dataset = c(
    "Training",
    "Testing"
  ),
  Observations = c(
    length(train_id),
    length(test_id)
  )
)

render_table(
  split_summary,
  "Number of observations in training and testing set",
  "cc"
)

```

### Feature Scaling

Continuous predictors were standardized by subtracting the training-set mean and dividing by the corresponding training-set standard deviation. The scaling parameters were estimated solely from the training data and then applied unchanged to the test data. This procedure prevents information from the holdout set from influencing model construction and ensures an unbiased evaluation of predictive performance.

```{r scaling, include = FALSE}

x_raw <- as.matrix(analysis_data[, predictors, drop = FALSE])
y_raw <- analysis_data[[config$response]]

x_prep <- fit_scaler(x_raw[train_id, , drop = FALSE])
x_raw_scaled <- apply_scaler(x_raw, x_prep)

x_train <- x_raw_scaled[train_id, , drop = FALSE]
x_test <- x_raw_scaled[test_id, , drop = FALSE]
y_train <- y_raw[train_id]
y_test <- y_raw[test_id]

```

### Five-fold Cross-validation

Five-fold cross-validation was performed using the training data only. Fold assignments were generated with the random seed *`r seeds$cv`*, ensuring reproducibility across all regression models. The same fold allocation was used throughout the analysis to provide a fair comparison between OLS, Ridge Regression, and Lasso Regression.

```{r cv, echo = FALSE}
foldid <- make_foldid(
    n = nrow(x_train),
    k = 5L,
    seed = seeds$cv
)

cv_summary <- tibble::tibble(
  Fold = "Number of Observations",
  Fold_1 = sum(foldid == 1),
  Fold_2 = sum(foldid == 2),
  Fold_3 = sum(foldid == 3),
  Fold_4 = sum(foldid == 4),
  Fold_5 = sum(foldid == 5)
)

render_table(
  cv_summary,
  caption = "Number of observations in each folds"
)

```

### Missing Value Handling

```{r missing value, echo = FALSE}

