### Explanation

The correlation analysis identified several moderately to highly correlated predictor pairs, most notably gleason and pgg45 (r=0.78), lcavol and lcp (r=0.72), and lcp and svi (r=0.67). These variables measure related clinical characteristics and therefore contain overlapping information. In an ordinary least squares model, such multicollinearity can increase the variance of the estimated coefficients, making them more sensitive to sampling variation even when prediction accuracy remains acceptable.

Ridge regression addresses this issue through an $L_2$ penalty that shrinks correlated coefficients simultaneously instead of eliminating individual predictors. Unlike variable-selection methods, Ridge retains every predictor in the model while reducing the magnitude of the estimated coefficients. Consequently, information shared among correlated variables is distributed across multiple predictors rather than being assigned to a single variable.

This behavior is evident when comparing the estimated coefficients under the two tuning rules. At \lambda_{min}, the dominant predictor lcavol has an estimated coefficient of 0.6212, while lcp, another highly correlated predictor, has a coefficient of −0.0898. When the penalty increases to \lambda_{1se}, the coefficient of lcavol decreases substantially to 0.3287, whereas the coefficient of lcp changes toward zero, from −0.0898 to 0.0930. Similarly, the coefficients of svi decrease from 0.2167 to 0.1498, lbph from 0.1889 to 0.1141, age from −0.1747 to −0.0393, and lweight from 0.1672 to 0.1263. Even predictors that are strongly correlated, such as gleason and pgg45, remain in the model under both tuning rules rather than being discarded.

