### Interpretation

Lasso regression produced different subsets of predictors under the two tuning rules. At $\lambda_{min}$, the model retained all eight predictors, indicating that each variable contributed useful predictive information when the primary objective was to minimize the cross-validation error. This result suggests that tumour characteristics, pathological indicators, and patient-specific factors all provide complementary information for predicting the logarithm of prostate-specific antigen (PSA).

Applying the more conservative one-standard-error rule resulted in a substantially simpler model containing only five predictors: lcavol, lweight, lbph, svi, and pgg45. These variables represent three major aspects of prostate cancer. lcavol (log cancer volume) remained the dominant predictor with the largest coefficient (0.5628), indicating that tumour volume is the strongest determinant of PSA level. svi (seminal vesicle invasion) and pgg45 (percentage of Gleason grades 4 or 5) describe tumour aggressiveness and pathological severity, suggesting that disease progression contributes important predictive information beyond tumour size alone. In contrast, lweight and lbph capture patient-specific physiological characteristics that influence PSA measurements independently of cancer severity.

The predictors age, lcp, and gleason, which were retained under $\lambda_{min}$, were excluded under $\lambda_{1SE}$. Their exclusion does not imply that these variables are clinically unimportant. Instead, Lasso removed them because their predictive information is partially represented by other retained predictors. For example, gleason is strongly correlated with pgg45, while lcp is moderately to strongly correlated with both lcavol and svi. When several predictors convey similar information, the $L_1$ penalty encourages a sparse representation by selecting only a subset of the correlated variables. Consequently, the resulting model is easier to interpret while maintaining competitive predictive performance.

Overall, the $\lambda_{min}$ model prioritizes predictive accuracy by retaining all available information, whereas the $\lambda_{1SE}$ model achieves greater interpretability by reducing the predictor set from eight variables to five with only a modest increase in cross-validation error. For applications where model simplicity and clinical interpretability are important, the $\lambda_{1SE}$ solution provides a more parsimonious and practically attractive model.

