## 4B. Elastic net on original predictors

```{r original-elastic-net, echo=FALSE}
alpha_grid <- c(0.10, 0.25, 0.50, 0.75, 0.90)

# Fit one cv.glmnet object per alpha with the same foldid.
# Select alpha and lambda using training CV only.

fit_elastic_net_4B <- function(x, y, foldid, alpha_list){
  fits <- list()
  for (alpha in alpha_list){
        fits[[as.character((alpha))]] <- cv.glmnet(
        x = x,
        y = y,
        alpha = alpha,
        foldid = foldid,
        standardize = FALSE
    )
  }
  return(fits)
}

elastic_net_list <- fit_elastic_net_4B(x_train, y_train, foldid, alpha_grid)

for (alpha in names(elastic_net_list)) {
  fit <- elastic_net_list[[alpha]]
  print(plot_coef_path_4B(fit, alpha, "Elastic Net Coefficient Paths"))
  print(plot_nonzero_slopes_4B(fit, alpha, "Number of Non-Zero Slopes"))
  print(plot_cross_validation_error(fit, alpha, "Cross-Validation Error"))
}
```

Overall, smaller values of $\alpha$ allow $\lambda$ to increase to larger values before all coefficients are shrunk to zero. Across the coefficient paths, **lcavol** consistently has the largest coefficient magnitude, suggesting that it is the most influential predictor of the response. In contrast, the coefficients for **age** and **lcp** are negative when $\lambda$ is small and are shrunk to zero much earlier than the other predictors as $\lambda$ increases, indicating that they contribute relatively little to predicting the response. As $\lambda$ increases, the number of non-zero coefficients decreases due to stronger regularization. Beyond the optimal value of $\lambda$, the cross-validation error increases because excessive regularization causes the model to underfit the data.

```{r best elastic-net, echo=FALSE}
